{
  "dom_id": "step-q94661",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Let $F$ be the set of polynomials of the form $ax+b,$ where $a,b\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The zero-polynomial belongs to $F.$\n- For the zero polynomial $0$ and any $p(x)\\in F,$ we have $p(x)+0=p(x).$\n- There are polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in F$ such that $p(x)+q(x)\\ne q(x)+p(x).$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the zero-polynomial can be represented as\n\n$$\n0=0\\cdot x+0.\n$$\n\nHence, $0\\in F.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for the zero-polynomial $0$ and any $p(x)\\in F,$ we have\n\n$$\np(x)+0=p(x)\n$$\n\nsince $0$ is the unique zero element in the set of all polynomials.\n\n- Statement III is false. For any polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in F,$ we have that\n\n$$\np(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x)\n$$\n\nsince the addition of polynomials is commutative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
